/-! # The coordinate Euler polynomial of a projective scheme over a field

Source: Stacks Project, varieties.tex Tags 0BEM and 08AE, constructions.tex Tag 03GL,
and coherent.tex Tag 0AG7 (blueprint: lem-sfb-projective-field-coordinate-euler-polynomial).

The ambient scheme is polynomial Proj. Its `Proj.twist` is the standard projective
space twist: on the degree-one charts it has the transition functions specified in
`def-pc-projective-space-twist`, including for negative integers.
-/

@[expose] public section
set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A closed projective scheme over a field is proper and Noetherian with coherent
structure sheaf. Its unique all-integer Euler polynomial is numerical, and is also
uniquely determined by the eventual graded dimensions of every defining homogeneous
ideal. An ideal defines the same closed subscheme precisely when its quotient Proj
is isomorphic to X over the fixed ambient projective space.
Source: Stacks Project, Tags 0BEM, 08AE, 03GL and 0AG7
(blueprint: lem-sfb-projective-field-coordinate-euler-polynomial).

Proof: (1) The finitely many standard degree-one affine charts cover the ambient
Proj. Their inverse images under the closed immersion are affine, so X is
quasi-compact. The polynomial scalar-extension isomorphism of
lem-weil-pbc-polynomial-scalar-extension and lem-nagata-affine-proj-scalar-extension
identify this Proj with the base-change projective space of
 def-nagata-projective-space-base-change. Thus the structure map is projective
(def-pc-projective-morphism), hence proper by lem-pc-projective-proper.
(2) Spec k is Noetherian, so finite type and lem-weil-pbc-finite-type-over-noetherian
make X Noetherian. The structure sheaf is quasi-coherent and generated by 1;
pqf-lem-coherent-ft-locnoeth makes it coherent.
(3) Put L = i^* O(1). The chart bases of def-pc-projective-space-twist and
 def-weil-pbc-proj-twisting-sheaf identify L's integer tensor powers with i^* O(d),
using dual bases for negative d (def-pc-module-pullback, def-sc-invertible-module).
(4) Apply lem-sc-snapper-numerical-polynomial with F = O_X and r = 1. The tensor
unit and (3) give the required Euler values at every integer, hence numericality.
(5) lem-sfb-projective-closed-subscheme-homogeneous-ideal gives a homogeneous ideal
presenting X over this ambient Proj, including when X is empty.
(6) For every such J, lem-sfb-projective-coordinate-ring-euler-polynomial identifies
its eventual graded dimensions with these Euler values. The isomorphism over the
ambient space identifies the pulled-back twists. No saturation is needed.
(7) Apply lem-sfb-projective-twist-euler-section-uniqueness to O_X, which has the
finite presentation O^0 -> O -> O -> 0. It identifies eventual Euler values with
section dimensions and gives uniqueness of their rational polynomial. Taking the
maximum of thresholds also proves uniqueness from any coordinate quotient. This
argument permits the unit ideal and the empty scheme throughout. -/
theorem exists_projectiveField_coordinateEulerPolynomial
    (k : Type u) [Field k] (n : ℕ) {X : Scheme.{u}}
    (i : X ⟶ Proj.projectiveSpaceOverAffine (ULift.{u} (Fin (n + 1))) k)
    [IsClosedImmersion i] :
    IsProper (i ≫ Proj.projectiveSpaceOverAffineToSpec (ULift.{u} (Fin (n + 1))) k) ∧
    IsNoetherian X ∧
    RingedSpace.IsCoherent X.toRingedSpace (SheafOfModules.unit X.ringCatSheaf) ∧
    ∃ Q : Polynomial ℚ,
      (∀ d : ℤ, Q.eval (d : ℚ) =
        (@Scheme.Modules.eulerChar k _ X
          (OverClass.ofHom (i ≫
            Proj.projectiveSpaceOverAffineToSpec (ULift.{u} (Fin (n + 1))) k))
          ((Scheme.Modules.tensorPullback i).obj
            (Proj.twist (MvPolynomial.homogeneousSubmodule (ULift.{u} (Fin (n + 1))) k) d)) :
          ℚ)) ∧
      (∀ R : Polynomial ℚ,
        (∀ d : ℤ, R.eval (d : ℚ) =
          (@Scheme.Modules.eulerChar k _ X
            (OverClass.ofHom (i ≫
              Proj.projectiveSpaceOverAffineToSpec (ULift.{u} (Fin (n + 1))) k))
            ((Scheme.Modules.tensorPullback i).obj
              (Proj.twist (MvPolynomial.homogeneousSubmodule (ULift.{u} (Fin (n + 1))) k) d)) :
            ℚ)) → R = Q) ∧
      (∀ d : ℤ, ∃ z : ℤ, Q.eval (d : ℚ) = (z : ℚ)) ∧
      (∃ J : HomogeneousIdeal
          (MvPolynomial.homogeneousSubmodule (ULift.{u} (Fin (n + 1))) k),
        ∃ e : Proj (coordinateQuotientPiece J) ≅ X,
          e.hom ≫ i = coordinateQuotientImmersion J) ∧
      (∀ J : HomogeneousIdeal
          (MvPolynomial.homogeneousSubmodule (ULift.{u} (Fin (n + 1))) k),
        (∃ e : Proj (coordinateQuotientPiece J) ≅ X,
          e.hom ≫ i = coordinateQuotientImmersion J) →
        (∃ b : ℕ, ∀ d : ℕ, b ≤ d →
          Q.eval (d : ℚ) = (Module.finrank k (coordinateQuotientPiece J d) : ℚ)) ∧
        (∀ R : Polynomial ℚ,
          (∃ b : ℕ, ∀ d : ℕ, b ≤ d →
            R.eval (d : ℚ) = (Module.finrank k (coordinateQuotientPiece J d) : ℚ)) →
          R = Q)) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
